VeridianOS: qbinaryjson: read the header from the array's own storage

GCC reported, in QBinaryJson::fromBinaryData()
(src/core5/serialization/qbinaryjson.cpp:165): "array subscript 'long
unsigned int[0]' is partly outside array bounds of 'const char [1]'
[-Warray-bounds]", the one-byte object being QByteArray::_empty.
QByteArray::constData() returns that shared empty string for a null
array; the function has already returned for any array shorter than the
header and base, so a null array never gets here, but GCC does not
connect size() with that branch and saw an 8-byte memcpy from a 1-byte
object.

The header and base are now read through data.constBegin(), which is the
array's own storage (constData() without the empty-string substitution)
and is the same pointer for any non-empty array. No behaviour change.


diff -ru a/src/core5/serialization/qbinaryjson.cpp b/src/core5/serialization/qbinaryjson.cpp
--- a/src/core5/serialization/qbinaryjson.cpp	2026-10-08 17:33:09.625858235 -0400
+++ b/src/core5/serialization/qbinaryjson.cpp	2026-10-08 17:33:23.650501558 -0400
@@ -161,10 +161,14 @@
     if (size_t(data.size()) < sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Header) + sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Base))
         return QJsonDocument();
 
+    // Non-empty, so constBegin() is the array's own storage (constData() may
+    // also return the one-byte shared empty string, which the compiler cannot
+    // rule out here).
+    const char *raw = data.constBegin();
     QBinaryJsonPrivate::Header h;
-    memcpy(&h, data.constData(), sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Header));
+    memcpy(&h, raw, sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Header));
     QBinaryJsonPrivate::Base root;
-    memcpy(&root, data.constData() + sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Header),
+    memcpy(&root, raw + sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Header),
            sizeof(QBinaryJsonPrivate::Base));
 
     uint size = 0;
